# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — system-wide KWin defaults
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   Factory settings for KWin — the part of KDE that draws every window, runs
#   the Overview, and owns the virtual desktops. Like its neighbour `kdeglobals`
#   in this same folder, everything here is a DEFAULT and never a forced value:
#   the moment a user changes any of it in System Settings, their choice is
#   written to their own ~/.config/kwinrc, which sits ABOVE this file in KDE's
#   search order and wins from then on, permanently.
#
#   The full explanation of that search order — and the warning about the tiny
#   zz-aquarius.sh script that makes this folder count for anything at all — is
#   at the top of `kdeglobals`, right next to this file. Read that one first if
#   any of this is new.
#
# WHY KWIN READS THIS FILE AT ALL
#   KWin opens its settings with KSharedConfig::openConfig("kwinrc") and no
#   extra flags. "No extra flags" is KConfig's normal mode: walk every folder
#   listed in XDG_CONFIG_DIRS, top to bottom, first answer wins. That is the
#   exact same mode kdeglobals is read in, so a kwinrc parked in our folder is
#   picked up the exact same way.
#     Source: kwin/src/main.cpp, Plasma/6.7 branch, line 84 —
#     https://invent.kde.org/plasma/kwin/-/blob/Plasma/6.7/src/main.cpp
#
# WHY IT STILL WORKS ON A WAYLAND LOGIN
#   Worth spelling out, because the ordering is not obvious. When you pick
#   "Plasma (Wayland)" at the login screen, the program that actually starts is
#   `startplasma-wayland`, and it does these things in this order:
#     1. runEnvironmentScripts()  — runs our zz-aquarius.sh, which is what puts
#                                   /usr/share/aquarius/xdg into XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
#     2. setupPlasmaEnvironment() — adds the user's own kdedefaults folder above ours
#     3. syncDBusEnvironment()    — hands that finished environment to systemd
#     4. startPlasmaSession()     — only NOW is kwin_wayland started
#   So KWin is launched with our folder already in its search path and reads
#   this file on its very first pass. Nothing has to be reloaded or restarted.
#     Source: plasma-workspace/startkde/startplasma-wayland.cpp (Plasma/6.7),
#     main() lines 66, 72, 87.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE SHIPS
#   Two things, in two halves.
#
#   1. The behaviour half of the "GNOME flow on KDE" work: a hot corner that
#      opens the Overview, and a strip of four workspaces for it to show. The
#      full plain-English write-up — including the one piece that could NOT be
#      done from this file, and why — is in:
#        ../../../../../docs/gnome-flow-behavior-layer.md
#
#   2. The glass: frosted, 16px-rounded application windows, switched on and
#      tuned in the three sections added lower down. Those depend on two
#      add-ons that are COMPILED INTO THIS IMAGE by
#      ../../../../../build_files/kwin-effects.sh — settings alone cannot
#      produce them, because KDE on its own will not blur behind a window that
#      did not ask. Why they are compiled here rather than installed as a
#      package, and how to bump their versions:
#        ../../../../../docs/kwin-effects-layer.md
#
#   The window SHADOW that goes with the glass is not set here. Shadows belong
#   to the window decoration, not the compositor, so they live one file over:
#   `breezerc`, next to this one.
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WORKSPACES — four of them, in a single row
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GNOME lays its workspaces out as one horizontal strip. KDE's virtual desktops
# are arranged on a grid, so "one row" is how you say "a strip" here. Four is
# the number: enough to think in, few enough to hold in your head.
#
# This also decides what the touchpad does. KWin only switches desktops sideways
# if the grid is more than one column wide, and only up-and-down if it is more
# than one row tall. Number=4 with Rows=1 gives a grid 4 wide and 1 tall, so a
# three-finger swipe left or right moves between workspaces and a three-finger
# swipe up or down correctly does nothing. That is exactly the GNOME feel.
#   Source: kwin/src/virtualdesktops.cpp (Plasma/6.7) — `grid().width() > 1`
#   and `grid().height() > 1` guards around the swipe handlers, lines 795–823.
#
# KWin's own defaults are Number=1 and Rows=2, so both lines below are doing
# real work.
#   Source: kwin/src/virtualdesktops.cpp (Plasma/6.7),
#   VirtualDesktopManager::load() — readEntry("Number", 1), readEntry("Rows", 2).
#
# Not set here on purpose: Name_1…Name_4 and Id_1…Id_4. Leaving the names out
# gets the translated "Desktop 1…4" for free in every language, and leaving the
# IDs out lets KWin mint fresh ones per machine, which is what it wants.
[Desktops]
Number=4
Rows=1

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOT CORNER — throw the mouse into the top-left corner to open the Overview
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GNOME's other famous entrance, and one of the two ways into the Overview on
# AquariusOS (the other is the Super key — see the note at the bottom of this
# file).
#
# BorderActivate is a list of screen edges, written as numbers. The numbers are
# positions on a clock face starting at the top and going clockwise:
#
#     0 = top          4 = bottom
#     1 = top-right    5 = bottom-left
#     2 = right        6 = left
#     3 = bottom-right 7 = TOP-LEFT   ← the one we want
#
#   Source: the ElectricBorder list in kwin/src/effect/globals.h (Plasma/6.7),
#   lines 75–86. The order of that list IS the numbering — the first name is 0.
#
# HONESTY NOTE: 7 is already KWin's own out-of-the-box default for this setting
# in Plasma 6.7, so on today's Bazzite this line changes nothing. It is written
# down anyway for two reasons: it states our intent in a place a person can read
# it, and it pins the behaviour if a future Plasma or a future Bazzite ever
# changes its mind. This is the cheap kind of insurance.
#   Source: the <default>ElectricTopLeft</default> on the BorderActivate entry in
#   kwin/src/plugins/overview/overviewconfig.kcfg (Plasma/6.7).
[Effect-overview]
BorderActivate=7

# ==============================================================================
# THE GLASS — which desktop effects run
# ==============================================================================
# This is the switchboard for the AquariusOS look: frosted-glass application
# windows with 16px rounded corners. Three switches, and the reason all three
# are needed is that we are REPLACING one of KDE's own effects rather than
# adding to it.
#
# HOW THE NAME OF A SWITCH IS WORKED OUT — it is not written down anywhere as a
# word, it is assembled in code, exactly like `gamecontrollerEnabled` in the
# handheld file next door. KWin reads this `[Plugins]` group and, for every
# effect it can find, looks for a key made of that effect's id with the word
# "Enabled" stuck on the end:
#
#     const QString key = effectName + QStringLiteral("Enabled");
#
#   Source: KDE/kwin, Plasma/6.7, src/effect/effectloader.cpp,
#   AbstractEffectLoader::readConfig(), lines 52–57. The effectName handed to it
#   is `effect.pluginId()` — same file, line 396.
#
# And an effect's id is simply the name of its plug-in FILE without the ".so".
# KWin finds those files by scanning one folder,
# /usr/lib64/qt6/plugins/kwin/effects/plugins/ (src/effect/effectloader.cpp,
# PluginEffectLoader::findAllEffects()).
#
# ⚠️ A misspelled key here does NOTHING and looks exactly like a key that works.
# Every one of the three below was read out of the project's own build file
# rather than copied off a forum, and the working is shown. It should still be
# confirmed once on a real machine — see the note at the end of this section.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[Plugins]

# 1. TURN OFF KDE's OWN BLUR.
#    Better Blur DX is a fork of this effect, not a companion to it. Upstream's
#    README is blunt about running both: "Some windows might get double blurred
#    and look off". So KDE's goes off and ours goes on, in that order.
#
#    id `blur`, from KDE/kwin, Plasma/6.7, src/plugins/blur/CMakeLists.txt —
#    `kwin_add_builtin_effect(blur ${blur_SOURCES})`.
#
#    This line is doing real work: KDE ships blur switched ON
#    (src/plugins/blur/metadata.json, "EnabledByDefault": true), so without it
#    both effects would run at once.
blurEnabled=false

# 2. TURN ON BETTER BLUR DX — the glass itself.
#    Stock KWin will never blur behind an application window unless the
#    application asks for it, and almost none do. This one can blur behind
#    everything, which is the difference between glass and cheap transparency.
#
#    id `better_blur_dx`, from the plug-in file better_blur_dx.so. That name
#    comes from xarblu/kwin-effects-better-blur-dx v2.5.1, src/CMakeLists.txt —
#    `add_library(better_blur_dx MODULE ${better_blur_dx_SOURCES})` — and there
#    is no "lib" on the front of it because KDE's shared CMake rules strip the
#    prefix off plug-ins: extra-cmake-modules, kde-modules/KDECMakeSettings.cmake,
#    `set(CMAKE_SHARED_MODULE_PREFIX "")` under the comment "in KDE plugins
#    don't have a prefix".
#
#    Confirmed a second way, from outside the source: upstream issue #105 quotes
#    a packager's build log naming the file
#    …/qt6/plugins/kwin-x11/effects/plugins/better_blur_dx.so (that is their X11
#    build; ours is the kwin/ folder — the file name is the same).
#
#    The effect ships switched OFF ("EnabledByDefault": false in its
#    src/metadata.json), so this line is what starts it.
better_blur_dxEnabled=true

# 3. TURN ON ROUNDED CORNERS.
#    id `kwin4_effect_shapecorners`, from matinlotfali/KDE-Rounded-Corners
#    v0.10.0, src/CMakeLists.txt —
#    `kcoreaddons_add_plugin(kwin4_effect_shapecorners …)`. The project's own
#    Fedora packaging lists the same file name literally, in
#    .copr/kwin-effect-roundcorners.spec:
#      %{_kf6_qtplugindir}/kwin/effects/plugins/kwin4_effect_shapecorners.so
#
#    This project DOES ship itself switched on ("EnabledByDefault": true), so
#    strictly this line changes nothing today. It is written anyway, for the
#    same reason the hot corner above is: it states our intent where a person
#    can read it, and it pins the behaviour if upstream ever changes its mind.
kwin4_effect_shapecornersEnabled=true

# ⚠️ STILL TO CONFIRM ON A REAL MACHINE (both ids above). The derivation is
# sound and doubly sourced, but the honest test is one command on a booted
# AquariusOS, which will list every effect KWin actually loaded:
#
#     qdbus org.kde.KWin /Effects loadedEffects
#
# `better_blur_dx` and `kwin4_effect_shapecorners` should both appear, and
# `blur` should not. If an id turns out to differ, correct it here — do not
# "work around" it by enabling things by hand in System Settings, because that
# writes into the user's own file and hides the problem from everybody else.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BLUR ITSELF — Better Blur DX settings
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THIS SECTION NAME COMES FROM. It is not "Effect-better_blur_dx" (the
# id with underscores) and it is not something we chose. The project spells it
# out, hyphens and all, in its settings description file:
#     <group name="Effect-better-blur-dx">
#   Source: xarblu/kwin-effects-better-blur-dx v2.5.1, src/blur.kcfg.
#
# And the file those settings are read FROM is this one, kwinrc:
#   src/blur.cpp line 131      — BlurConfig::instance(effects->config())
#                                (KWin's own config, which is kwinrc)
#   src/kcm/blur_config.cpp:27 — BlurConfig::instance("kwinrc")
#                                (the System Settings page, spelled out)
#
# Every default quoted below is from that same blur.kcfg, so it is always clear
# which of these lines is actually changing something.
[Effect-better-blur-dx]

# HOW STRONG THE BLUR IS. 1–15. Not a pixel radius: it is the number of steps in
# a repeated shrink-and-grow ("Kawase") blur, so each step roughly doubles the
# softness and the scale is nothing like CSS's blur(20px). 11 is the setting
# that reads closest to the design's 20–24px frosted glass. Tuned by eye, on
# purpose — there is no honest arithmetic that converts between the two.
# (Upstream default: 15, which is the maximum and softer than we want.)
BlurStrength=11

# GRAIN, OFF. This adds fine noise over the blurred area to hide the banding
# that smooth gradients can show. Our design's surfaces are flat colour over
# blur with no long gradients, so the grain has nothing to hide and only makes
# text look slightly dirty. (Upstream default: 5.)
NoiseStrength=0

# COLOUR LIFT. The design asks for saturate(1.3) behind glass — the wallpaper
# showing through stays vivid instead of going grey. 130 is that 1.3, written
# as a percentage. (Upstream default: 150, which is stronger than the design.)
Saturation=130

# ROUND THE BLUR TO MATCH THE WINDOW. Without this the blur is drawn as a plain
# rectangle and its square corners poke out past the window's rounded ones —
# upstream calls this the "korners" bug by name. 16 matches the 16px corner
# radius set for the windows themselves further down this file, and the two
# numbers must be changed together or the corners stop lining up.
# (Upstream default: 0, i.e. square.)
CornerRadius=16

# BLUR BEHIND THE TITLEBAR AND BORDERS TOO. The window frame is drawn by Breeze,
# which has no transparency setting of its own; without this line the glass
# stops at the titlebar and every window wears an opaque hat.
# (Upstream default: false.)
BlurDecorations=true

# MENUS AND PANELS COUNT AS WINDOWS. Both are excluded from blurring by default.
# "Docks" here means panels — our top bar and dock — and their glass is the
# single most visible surface in the design, so both are switched on.
# (Upstream defaults: both false.)
#
# Read upstream's own note before assuming these do more than they do: they only
# ALLOW these window types to be blurred. What actually decides it is the
# matching rule immediately below. (docs/configuration.md, "Blur menus / Blur
# docks".)
BlurMenus=true
BlurDocks=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "BLUR EVERY WINDOW" — and how to read this switch, because it is backwards
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Better Blur DX keeps a list of window classes and one switch that decides what
# the list MEANS. In System Settings the switch is a pair of radio buttons:
#
#     ( ) Blur only matching        ← kcfg_BlurMatching
#     ( ) Blur all except matching  ← kcfg_BlurNonMatching
#
#   Source: src/kcm/blur_config.ui, the two QRadioButtons in "Force blur".
#
# So "blur everything" is the SECOND one, with an empty list: blur all windows
# except the ones named, and name none. Which means the line that does the work
# is `BlurMatching=false` — a false that switches something ON. It is worth
# reading twice.
#
# THE POLARITY, TRACED THROUGH THE SOURCE rather than assumed
# (src/window_manager.cpp, v2.5.1):
#
#   reconfigure(), lines 145–149:
#       if (config->blurMatching()) m_windowClassMatchMode = Whitelist;
#       else                        m_windowClassMatchMode = Blacklist;
#
#   shouldForceBlurWindowClass(), lines 249–267 — the full truth table:
#       window IS in the list  + Whitelist → blur it
#       window IS in the list  + Blacklist → do not blur it
#       window NOT in the list + Whitelist → do not blur it
#       window NOT in the list + Blacklist → BLUR IT      ← what we want
#
# Note that only `BlurMatching` is ever read by the effect; `BlurNonMatching`
# appears nowhere in the effect's code. It exists so the second radio button has
# a setting of its own to bind to. It is written below anyway, so that a user
# opening the settings page sees the correct button already selected instead of
# neither.
BlurMatching=false
BlurNonMatching=true

# The list itself: empty, so nothing is excluded.
#
# It has to be written out as an empty value rather than left out, because a
# missing key means "use the upstream default" — and upstream's default is the
# placeholder text `class1 / class2 / class3`. (Harmless either way in this
# mode: three imaginary applications would be excluded from blurring. Written
# properly regardless, so the settings page shows an empty box.)
WindowClasses=

# DELIBERATELY NOT SET: ForceContrastParams.
#   Turning it on would make the Saturation above apply to Plasma's own surfaces
#   — the top bar, the dock, popups — overriding the values our Plasma Style
#   sets for each of them individually in its plasmarc. Those per-surface values
#   are the more precise answer and they are the Plasma Style's job, so this
#   effect is left respecting them, which is its default.
#     Source: docs/configuration.md, "Force Contrast Parameters", which points
#     at libplasma's own per-theme plasmarc as the thing being respected.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT SET: the whole Refraction group (RefractionStrength and
#   friends). It is a glass-edge distortion effect, it costs GPU time, and it is
#   not in the AquariusOS design. Off is upstream's default.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROUNDED CORNERS — KDE-Rounded-Corners settings
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Section name and config file both read out of the project's own settings
# description: src/kcm/options.kcfg has <kcfgfile name="kwinrc"/> at the top and
# <group name="Round-Corners"> around everything below it.
#   Source: matinlotfali/KDE-Rounded-Corners v0.10.0.
[Round-Corners]

# 16px corners on the focused window and on every other one. Two keys because
# the effect can round the inactive window differently; the design does not, so
# they are the same number. Both must match the CornerRadius set for the blur
# above. (Upstream defaults: 20 and 20.)
Size=16
InactiveCornerRadius=16

# A TRUE CIRCLE, NOT AN APPLE-STYLE SQUIRCLE. Upstream defaults to the squircle
# — the continuous-curvature shape iOS uses — and it is a real, visible
# difference. The AquariusOS design's 16px radius is drawn as a plain rounded
# rectangle, and the window corners have to agree with the panel and popup
# corners the Plasma Style draws, which are also plain. (Upstream default: true.)
UseSquircleShape=false

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHADOWS — one shadow, not two
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The obvious trap with two effects that can both draw a shadow is drawing both,
# which gives every window a dark halo twice as heavy as intended.
#
# This one line avoids it. With it on, KDE-Rounded-Corners does NOT draw a
# shadow of its own: it takes the shadow the window's decoration already has —
# Breeze's, whose size and strength we set next door in `breezerc` — and
# re-shapes it to follow the rounded corners.
#
# It is one or the other, never both, and the shader says so plainly:
#     if (!isDrawingShadows())      … no shadow
#     else if (usesNativeShadows)   return getNativeShadow(…);
#     else                          return getCustomShadow(…);
#   Source: src/shaders/shapecorners_shadows.glsl, getShadow().
#
# True is also upstream's default; it is written out because it is the load-
# bearing half of a decision that spans two files, and a future reader changing
# breezerc needs to find this line.
UseNativeDecorationShadows=true

# DELIBERATELY NOT SET: this effect's OWN shadow settings — ShadowSize,
#   ShadowColor, ActiveShadowAlpha, InactiveShadowSize and the rest. Every one
#   of them is ignored while UseNativeDecorationShadows is true (see the shader
#   above), so writing them would be writing lines that do nothing, which is
#   worse than leaving them out. The window shadow is tuned in ONE place:
#   ../xdg/breezerc.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WINDOW OUTLINES — judged on a screen, 2026-08-30, and changed
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Until the first hardware boot these were left at upstream's defaults, on the
# honest grounds that numbers picked without looking at a screen are numbers
# nobody has checked. They have now been looked at, and they are wrong for us.
#
# WHAT UPSTREAM DRAWS BY DEFAULT, and what it looked like
#   Two rings around every window: a 1px BLACK one on the frame edge, and a 1px
#   WHITE one just inside it at a third opacity. Against the AquariusOS
#   wallpaper the black one all but disappears and the white one reads as a
#   cream hairline tracing every window — brighter than anything else on the
#   screen and completely unlike the design's near-invisible border.
#
# WHERE THE DEFAULTS COME FROM — matinlotfali/KDE-Rounded-Corners v0.10.0,
# src/kcm/options.kcfg (the file this whole [Round-Corners] group is described
# by; it carries <kcfgfile name="kwinrc"/> and <group name="Round-Corners">):
#
#   <entry name="OutlineThickness" type="Double"><default>1</default>
#                                   <min>0</min><max>20</max></entry>
#   <entry name="OutlineColor" type="Color"><default>black</default></entry>
#   <entry name="ActiveOutlineAlpha" type="Int"><default>255</default>
#                                   <min>0</min><max>255</max></entry>
#   <entry name="SecondOutlineThickness" type="Double"><default>1</default>
#                                   <min>0</min><max>20</max></entry>
#   <entry name="SecondOutlineColor" type="Color"><default>white</default></entry>
#   <entry name="ActiveSecondOutlineAlpha" type="Int"><default>85</default>
#                                   <min>0</min><max>255</max></entry>
#
# 85 out of 255 is the "33%" — there is no 0.33 anywhere in the project; the
# opacity is always a whole number out of 255.
#
# HOW COLOUR AND OPACITY ARE WRITTEN, because it is not what you would guess
#   The colour keys hold RGB only. Whatever alpha you write into them is read
#   and then thrown away, and replaced by the separate Alpha key:
#
#     color.setAlpha(alpha);      // src/WindowConfig.cpp, resolveColor()
#
#   So `OutlineColor=237,239,247,36` would NOT give you a 14% line — it would
#   give you a solid one. The opacity has to go in ActiveOutlineAlpha.
#
#   The other trap: ACTIVE has no prefix and INACTIVE does, but only on some of
#   them. `OutlineColor` is the active colour; `InactiveOutlineColor` is the
#   inactive one; there is no such key as `ActiveOutlineColor`. The alphas, by
#   contrast, are BOTH prefixed. Every key below was copied out of the kcfg
#   rather than guessed for exactly this reason.
#
# WHAT WE SHIP
#   One ring, at the design's hairline. `border-2` in branding/tokens.md is
#   rgba(237,239,247,.14) — the same line the dock is drawn with — and 14% of
#   255 is 35.7, which rounds to 36. Inactive windows drop to the top bar's
#   quieter 10%, which is 25.5 → 26; a window you are not using should recede.
OutlineThickness=1
InactiveOutlineThickness=1
OutlineColor=237,239,247
InactiveOutlineColor=237,239,247
ActiveOutlineAlpha=36
InactiveOutlineAlpha=26

#   And no second ring at all. The design's 7% inner highlight is a TOP-edge
#   sheen — the Plasma Style draws it that way and fades it out round the
#   corners — but this effect can only draw its inner line as a complete ring.
#   A 7% ring all the way round is not the design; it is a different, quieter
#   version of the cream line we are removing. Off is the honest answer.
#
#   Zero really does switch it off rather than draw a hairline-thin one; the
#   shader guards every outline pass on it:
#     bool hasSecondOutline() { return secondOutlineColor1.a > 0.0
#                                   && secondOutlineThickness > 0.0; }
#     Source: v0.10.0, src/shaders/variables.glsl.
#   And <min>0</min> on the entry means 0 is a legal value, not a clamped one.
SecondOutlineThickness=0
InactiveSecondOutlineThickness=0

# DELIBERATELY NOT SET: OuterOutlineThickness / InactiveOuterOutlineThickness.
#   This is a THIRD ring, drawn outside the window into the shadow margin.
#   Upstream ships it at 0 — off — which is what we want, and repeating a
#   default that is already correct is noise.
#
# ⚠️ STILL OPEN: the dark line that runs past the top-left corner.
#   The bench photo shows, at the top-left corner of a window, a straight dark
#   1px line carrying on upwards past the point where the rounded corner
#   curves away, ending abruptly against the wallpaper. Changing the outline
#   colour above may or may not remove it, and it is worth writing down why
#   the two possibilities are hard to tell apart from a photograph.
#
#   It could be this outline drawn on the square frame rather than the rounded
#   one. Against that: the effect computes the outline and the window edge from
#   the same rounded distance field (shapeCorner() in src/shaders/
#   shapecorners.glsl), so a mismatch would show at every corner in both
#   directions — and the photo has a vertical stub with no horizontal twin.
#
#   The likelier answer is the corner reconstruction that comes with
#   UseNativeDecorationShadows=true, set above. In that mode the effect does
#   not draw a shadow; it rebuilds what should sit behind the corner it just
#   rounded off, by sampling the window texture about three pixels OUTSIDE the
#   frame on each side and blending the two samples across the corner
#   (getNativeShadow(), src/shaders/shapecorners_shadows.glsl). Three pixels
#   outside the frame is where the window decoration paints its own dark
#   border, so that dark pixel gets smeared along the corner — and because the
#   sampled region starts two pixels outside the frame, the smear genuinely
#   extends past the window edge onto the wallpaper, exactly as photographed.
#
#   Upstream agrees. Issue #252, "Remnant of outline despite it being disabled
#   in the settings" — reporter has BOTH outlines switched off and still sees
#   a stub at the top-left; the maintainer's reply is "Actually, it is not the
#   outline, but the shadow", and adds that it appears on Wayland and not on
#   X11. Issue #378 is the same artefact at 125% display scaling.
#
#   CONCLUSION: it is most likely the shadow reconstruction, not the outline.
#   It is NOT being changed here, because the fix — UseNativeDecorationShadows
#   =false — undoes the deliberate one-shadow decision above and would let this
#   effect draw its own shadow alongside Breeze's. That trade needs a screen,
#   not a guess. THE NEXT BENCH TEST, in order:
#     1. Look again with the outline colour changed above. If the stub is gone,
#        it was the outline and there is nothing left to do.
#     2. If it is still there, try UseNativeDecorationShadows=false together
#        with ShadowSize / ActiveShadowAlpha tuned to match breezerc, and check
#        the window shadow has not doubled.
#     3. Check the display scaling. If it is 125% or 150%, try 100% once — #378
#        says the sample offsets land wrong at fractional scales.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT set here
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [ModifierOnlyShortcuts] — the old way to make the Super key on its own do
#   something. On Plasma 5 you wrote a line like
#       Meta=org.kde.kglobalaccel,/component/kwin,...,invokeShortcut,Overview
#   in this section, and KWin obeyed it. That whole feature was DELETED in
#   Plasma 6.1. The source files that implemented it (modifier_only_shortcuts.cpp
#   and .h) exist in KWin's Plasma/5.27 branch and are simply gone from Plasma/6.7
#   — nothing anywhere in modern KWin reads this section. Writing it here would
#   look like it worked and quietly do nothing, which is worse than not writing
#   it, so it is not written.
#
#   Where that setting lives now, and why it cannot live in this folder either,
#   is explained in full — with the exact replacement text — in
#   ../../../../../docs/gnome-flow-behavior-layer.md.
#
# Touchpad gestures — not configurable at all. KWin hard-codes which swipe does
#   what; there is no setting, no config file and no System Settings page for it
#   in Plasma 6.7. What the gestures actually do is written up in the doc above
#   so the welcome screen can teach them correctly.
#
# Screen-edge actions for the other seven corners and edges — left alone. The
#   top-left corner is the only one we claim; everything else stays however
#   Plasma and Bazzite left it.
#
# [NightColor] Active — Night Light, the feature that warms the screen towards
#   orange after sunset. On a machine people grade video on, a screen that
#   quietly changes colour is a real problem, so this was investigated as part
#   of the Phase 2 "colour-managed desktop" work. Nothing needs writing: KWin
#   ships it switched OFF, and neither Bazzite nor Fedora turns it on.
#     Source: kwin/src/plugins/nightlight/nightlightsettings.kcfg (Plasma/6.7)
#     — <entry name="Active" type="Bool"><default>false</default>.
#   Anyone who wants it can switch it on in System Settings → Display & Monitor
#   → Night Light, and anyone grading should leave it alone. Written down here
#   rather than set, because a line that repeats an existing default is noise
#   unless it is buying insurance, and there is nothing here to insure against.
#
# Colour profiles (ICC) — also investigated, also nothing to ship, but for a
#   different reason: they are per-screen and per-machine. Plasma 6.7 has a full
#   colour-management stack and will happily use an ICC profile per display
#   (System Settings → Display & Monitor → Colour Profile), but the profile that
#   belongs to one monitor is meaningless on another, so there is no such thing
#   as a sensible factory default. KWin stores the choice in its per-output
#   configuration, not in this file, so it could not be shipped from here even
#   if it made sense to. The full reasoning is in docs/codec-layer.md.
